cli-entware-no-storage = Router { $router } cannot run Entware: it needs the USB and storage components.
cli-entware-unsaved = Router { $router } has unsaved changes: save or discard them before the Entware setup.
cli-entware-fail-safe-active = The fail-safe timer of router { $router } is armed: commit or roll back first.
cli-entware-arch-required = Router { $router } reports the mips architecture: choose --arch mipsel or --arch mips.
cli-entware-drive-not-eligible = Router { $router } cannot run Entware from this drive; `kcc entware status` lists the drives it can use.
cli-entware-disk-set = Entware is already set up on router { $router }: remove it first with `kcc entware remove`.
cli-entware-setup-progress = Entware setup { $current }%: { $what }
cli-entware-step-components = installing the components (the router restarts)
cli-entware-step-install = starting the installer
cli-entware-step-verify = waiting for the installer
cli-entware-step-read-back = reading the setting back
cli-entware-setup-done = Entware is installed on { $router }. The configuration is unsaved; run `kcc config save`
cli-entware-setup-failed = Entware setup job { $name } did not complete: { $reason }
cli-entware-name-invalid = The package or service name is not valid. Nothing was sent.
cli-entware-ssh-confirmation-invalid = The ticket must be the 64 hexadecimal digits `kcc entware ssh probe` printed, and the fingerprint SHA256: followed by 43 base64 characters. Nothing was sent.
cli-entware-ssh-chroot = opkg chroot is on for router { $router }, so kcc cannot manage Entware over SSH: turn it off with `kcc entware set chroot off`.
cli-entware-ssh-host-changed = The URL host of router { $router } changed since the enrolment and the pin was cleared: probe and bootstrap again.
cli-entware-ssh-not-enrolled = Entware SSH is not enrolled for router { $router }: run `kcc entware ssh probe`, then `kcc entware ssh bootstrap`.
cli-entware-ssh-repin-required = The Entware host key of router { $router } differs from the pinned one: check it out of band and run `kcc entware ssh repin`.
cli-entware-ssh-key-unchanged = The Entware host key of router { $router } is the pinned one: there is nothing to re-pin.
cli-entware-ssh-busy = Router { $router } is busy with another change or job: try again when it ends.
cli-entware-ssh-slow = Router { $router } did not answer over SSH in time: it is slow or busy. Try again later.
cli-entware-ssh-ticket-missing = There is no ticket for router { $router }: every attempt uses its ticket up. Run `kcc entware ssh probe` again.
cli-entware-ssh-ticket-expired = The ticket for router { $router } expired: run `kcc entware ssh probe` again.
cli-entware-ssh-ticket-fingerprint = The confirmed fingerprint is not the probed one for router { $router }: no password was sent and the ticket is used up. Check the fingerprint and probe again.
cli-entware-ssh-ticket-invalid = The ticket does not match router { $router }, its SSH port or this user: no password was sent and the ticket is used up. Probe again.
cli-entware-ssh-hostkey-mismatch = The Entware host key of router { $router } is now { $value }, not the pinned one: kcc sent nothing. If Entware was reinstalled, check the new fingerprint out of band and run `kcc entware ssh repin`.
cli-entware-ssh-auth-rejected = Entware on router { $router } refused the sign-in. After a failed bootstrap the enrolment stays Pending: probe and bootstrap again with the right password.
cli-entware-ssh-disabled = SSH management of Entware is off for router { $router }: run `kcc entware ssh enable`.
cli-entware-package-not-installed = The package { $name } is not installed on router { $router }; nothing was changed.
cli-entware-service-unknown = Router { $router } has no Entware service { $name }; nothing was changed.
cli-entware-service-disabled = The Entware service { $name } on router { $router } is disabled (its ENABLED is not yes); it is now { $value }.
cli-entware-ssh-active-notice = Router { $router } is enrolled. A new bootstrap or re-pin replaces kcc's key and marks the enrolment Pending before the router is contacted: with a wrong password or an unreachable router, SSH management stops until a bootstrap succeeds. Confirm with --yes.
cli-prompt-entware-password = Entware root password of router { $router }:
cli-entware-ssh-enabled = SSH management of Entware is on for router { $router }, port { $port }.
cli-entware-ssh-forgotten = kcc forgot its SSH key and pin for router { $router }. Its key line stays in the router's authorized_keys until it is removed there.
cli-entware-ssh-probe-key = Entware host key of router { $router }: { $value }
cli-entware-ssh-probe-ticket = Ticket (single use, valid for five minutes): { $item }
cli-entware-ssh-probe-check = Before you confirm, compare this fingerprint with the one the router itself shows, over a path you trust (for example the Entware console: dropbearkey -y -f /opt/etc/dropbear/dropbear_ed25519_host_key). Then run: kcc entware ssh bootstrap --ticket <ticket> --fingerprint <fingerprint> --confirm-destructive { $router }
cli-entware-ssh-enrolled = Entware SSH is enrolled for router { $router }: kcc pinned the host key { $value } and signs in with its own key from now on.
cli-entware-ssh-default-password = Warning: the Entware root password of router { $router } is still Entware's default: change it on the router.
cli-entware-ssh-revoked = kcc removed its key from router { $router } and forgot its SSH state.
cli-entware-packages-none = No packages.
cli-entware-packages-updated = The package lists of router { $router } are updated.
cli-entware-package-changed = { $name } on router { $router }: { $value } (before: { $item }).
cli-entware-package-absent = not installed
cli-entware-services-none = No Entware services.
cli-entware-service-running = running
cli-entware-service-stopped = stopped
cli-entware-service-state = { $name }: { $value }
cli-entware-service-log-empty = No log records of { $name }, or the router log could not be read.
